LibreForum Coding Standards
===========================

LibreForum is a fork of Phorum 5.2. The Phorum 5 standards below still apply
to style, with the following rules for NEW code. They take precedence over
points 1 and 2 of the Phorum section.

Why a separate prefix: LibreForum is covered by two licenses (see NOTICE).
Code inherited from Phorum is under the Phorum License 2.0 (LICENSE-PHORUM);
code written for LibreForum is under the Apache License 2.0 (LICENSE). A
distinct prefix makes that boundary visible in the code itself.

1. Naming new code

   - New functions and APIs:               libreforum_*       e.g. libreforum_<area>_<action>()
   - New constants:                        LIBREFORUM_*
   - Hook functions of a new module:       libreforum_mod_<module>_<hook>
     (the function name is declared in the module's info.txt, so any
     prefix works)
   - New settings: under $PHORUM['libreforum'][...] rather than new
     top-level keys.

   Keep the phorum_ prefix only when the new function fulfils an existing
   Phorum contract that other code calls by name: a database layer function
   (phorum_db_*), a member of an existing API family (phorum_api_*), a
   template or hook name expected by Phorum. Consistency of the family wins.

   Do NOT rename existing identifiers ($PHORUM, phorum_*(), PHORUM_*, the
   phorum_ table prefix). They do not name the product, and renaming them
   would break every existing module.

2. Header of a new file

   Do not copy the Phorum header into a new file: it would attribute your
   code to the Phorum Development Team and to the Phorum License. Use:

       <?php
       // LibreForum — https://github.com/fbastin/LibreForum
       // Copyright 2026 Fabian Bastin and the LibreForum contributors
       // SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

   Files inherited from Phorum keep their original header unchanged, even
   when you modify them: the Phorum License requires the copyright notice to
   be retained.

3. Site-specific code

   Code that only makes sense for one installation (paths, business rules,
   external services of a given site) does not belong on the main branch.
   Keep it on that site's branch, with that site's prefix.


Phorum 5 Coding Standards
=========================

For the most part, Phorum 5 uses the standards set forth in the PEAR Coding
Standards located at http://pear.php.net/manual/en/standards.php. We do
however differ slightly in a few of ways.

1. Header Comment - please use the header comment found in the other Phorum
   files.

2. Function Naming - PEAR uses the "studly caps" naming. We like to name
   functions in all lower case with _. So for example:  phorum_get_users().
   Don't worry about long names. It is better to have a function named
   well.

3. Variable Names - PEAR really does not address this as the code you are
   writing belongs to your package. However, in Phorum, the code you write
   belongs to us all. Please make variable names useful and clear. Avoid
   single character variables and things like $temp.

Now, in looking at the code, you will see that this is not being followed to
the letter. Sometimes, editors don't cooperate and you get tabs, other
times you just forget and don't do it right. Just don't be upset if you see
an SVN commit that just says "formatting" on your code. Some editors have
auto-format features and will be used to clean up formatting.

Even if you are not going to write a module, you may want to read the
creating_mods.txt file. It covers some general concepts that exist in
Phorum that may help you understand more of the Phorum code.

Additionally, if you will be using SVN please read the SVN-RULES file.
